<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Jolliet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1645">1645</a>–<a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Louis Joliet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Jolliet was a French-Canadian explorer and fur trader born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. In 1673, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-marquette">Jacques Marquette</a>, he led the first recorded European expedition down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, reaching the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> river and establishing that the river flowed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. He later explored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> and the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> and served as royal hydrographer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-jolliet.jpg" alt="Louis Jolliet" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Jolliet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Jolliet_Signature.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jolliet was born on 21 September 1645 at Beaupré, a French settlement near Quebec City, to Jean Jolliet and Marie d'Abancourt. His father died when he was six, and his mother married the merchant Geoffroy Guillot dit Lavalle, who owned land on the Île d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, an island in the Saint Lawrence home to First Nations. Jolliet spent much time there and probably began speaking Indigenous languages as a child; he also learned English and Spanish. He entered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> school in Quebec, studied philosophy and religion with the priesthood in view, and became a skilled harpsichordist and church organist. He received Holy Orders in 1662 but left the seminary in 1667 to take up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 17 May 1673 Jolliet and the Jesuit Jacques Marquette left St Ignace, Michigan, with two canoes and five voyageurs of French-Indian ancestry. They sailed to Green Bay, went up the Fox River to the site of present-day Portage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, and portaged less than two miles to the Wisconsin River. On 17 June they entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> near present-day Prairie du Chien, Wisconsin. They paddled along its west bank until mid-July, and on passing the mouth of the Arkansas River they were satisfied that the Mississippi flowed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. They had met natives carrying European goods and feared a hostile meeting with Spanish explorers or colonists.</p><p>They returned by the Illinois River, which friendly natives said was a shorter way, then the Des Plaines River and the Chicago Portage, reaching Lake Michigan near present-day Chicago. Marquette stayed at the mission at Green Bay, and Jolliet went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. In the Lachine Rapids his canoe overturned and his records were lost; his brief narrative, written from memory, agrees in essentials with Marquette's, the chief account of the journey. In 1680 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> granted him an island as a reward, in 1693 he became Royal Hydrographer, and in 1694 he sailed along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> coast as far as Zoar, recording the country, navigation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May 1700 Jolliet left for Anticosti Island and then disappears from the historical record. No record of his death or burial survives; the only trace of his fate is a notation that a Mass was said for his soul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> on 15 September 1700. His name is carried by the cities of Joliet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, and Joliette, Quebec, and by Joliet Junior College. He and Marquette appear on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> postage stamp of 1968.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis Jolliet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French explorers charted much of the interior: <b>Louis Jolliet</b> and Jacques Marquette descended the Mississippi in 1673, René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle reached its mouth in 1682 and claimed Louisiana for Louis XIV, and the La Vérendrye family pushed west towards the prairies in the 1730s and 1740s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Louis Jolliet</b> and the Jesuit Jacques Marquette left St Ignace with five companions in two canoes.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-17">17 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>When Jacques Marquette and <b>Louis Jolliet</b> visited in 1673, the Grand Village of the Kaskaskia near Starved Rock held thousands of people.</blockquote><a href="/state/illinois-confederation">Illinois Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Quebec City, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.82,-71.22&amp;year=1680" aria-label="Quebec City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis Jolliet"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4347 6062h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4347 6062h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.82,-71.22&amp;year=1680">What was here in 1680? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
